WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.424
Oceanic islands have life cycles.

00:00:02.424 --> 00:00:06.573
They are born, they grow, and then they slowly sink and wear away.

00:00:07.673 --> 00:00:13.343
In 1963 the geophysicist J. Tuzo Wilson explained why volcanic islands form chains that

00:00:13.343 --> 00:00:15.103
get older in one direction.

00:00:15.103 --> 00:00:20.447
A plume of hot mantle stays roughly fixed, while the tectonic plate moves over it.

00:00:20.447 --> 00:00:24.879
Each volcano grows over the plume, is carried away, cools and sinks.

00:00:25.979 --> 00:00:27.460
Why do old volcanoes sink?

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.592
The ocean plate becomes thicker and denser as it cools.

00:00:30.592 --> 00:00:35.661
And the weight of the volcano itself bends the plate downwards, a process called flexure.

00:00:35.661 --> 00:00:38.508
Flexure also pushes up a bulge some distance away.

00:00:38.508 --> 00:00:41.641
We will see that bulge lift Henderson Island in Unit 2.

00:00:42.741 --> 00:00:47.212
Charles Darwin saw coral reefs during the voyage of HMS Beagle,

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:50.903
and published his theory of how atolls form in 1842.

00:00:52.003 --> 00:00:56.391
In 1842, Charles Darwin argued that coral reefs record this sinking.

00:00:56.391 --> 00:01:00.585
Corals only grow in the sunlit top few tens of metres of the sea.

00:01:00.585 --> 00:01:05.103
So as a volcanic island sinks, its fringing reef keeps growing upward,

00:01:05.103 --> 00:01:09.685
becoming a barrier reef, and finally an atoll ring with no peak at all.

00:01:10.785 --> 00:01:14.495
Darwin's theory waited more than a century for proof.

00:01:14.495 --> 00:01:18.485
The Royal Society drilled Funafuti between 1896 and 1898,

00:01:18.485 --> 00:01:23.105
passing through about three hundred and forty metres of reef rock,

00:01:23.105 --> 00:01:25.135
without reaching the volcano.

00:01:26.235 --> 00:01:29.834
The decisive test came on Enewetak Atoll in the Marshall Islands.

00:01:30.934 --> 00:01:36.366
In 1952, boreholes there struck volcanic basalt beneath more than twelve hundred metres

00:01:36.366 --> 00:01:37.490
of reef limestone.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:38.551
Darwin was right.

00:01:38.551 --> 00:01:42.423
If reef growth cannot keep pace, the island drowns completely,

00:01:42.423 --> 00:01:45.295
leaving a flat-topped seamount called a guyot.
